The increased latency that we observed does not arise from lost ACKs, =20
but from the lack of enough packets in flight to be able to trigger =20
fast retransmits. This effectively limits the retransmission options =20
to retransmission by timeout, which again will increase exponentially =20
with each subsequent retransmissions. We have also found that the =20
"thin" stream patterns are very often generated by applications where =20
human interaction is the purpose. Such applications will give a =20
degraded experience to the user if such high latencies happen often. =20
In-depth discussion of these effects can be found in the papers I =20
linked to.
If the application produces less than one packet per RTT, the dupACK-=20
modification will be ineffective and any improved latency will be from =
=20
linear timeouts. If the number of packets in flight are 2-4, no fast =20
retransmissions may be triggered based on the 3 dupACK scheme, but a =20
retransmission upon the first indication of loss will improve =20
retransmission latency.

