The removal of exponential backoff on a general basis has been =20
investigated and discussed already, for instance here:
http://ccr.sigcomm.org/online/?q=3Dnode/416
Such steps are, however considered drastic, and I agree that caution =20
must be made to thoroughly investigate the effects of such changes.
The changes introduced by the proposed patches, however, are not =20
default behaviour, but an option for applications that suffer from the =
=20
thin-stream TCP increased retransmission latencies. They will, as =20
such, not affect all streams. In addition, the changes will only be =20
active for streams which are perpetually thin or in the early phase of =
=20
expanding their cwnd. Also, experiments performed on congested =20
bottlenecks with tail-drop queues show very little (if any at all) =20
effect on goodput for the modified scenario compared to a scenario =20
with unmodified TCP streams.

Graphs both for latency-results and fairness tests can be found here:
http://folk.uio.no/apetlund/lktmp/

